A||||23||kel||steering||||5||
Z||000000||kel||11-23-1999||06:02 PM||||I have several questions:<BR>(for the '86 SE 2.8L V6)<P>1.  does anyone know if there are different ratios for the manual steering rack?<P>2.  how many turns are there lock to lock?<P>3.  did they use different racks for different year Fieros?<P>4.  does anyone know the parts # for the rack used in the '86 V6 SE?<P>5.  what's the difference between the '86 GT and V6 SE?  (besides the body)<P>The steering is less responsive than it used to be and my mechanic and I are trying to come up with some solutions.  He just put on new shocks and struts and says the front ball joints look good.  The steering wasn't always like this.  It used to feel much quicker.  Any help would be appreciated.  Thanks.    ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Patrick||11-23-1999||06:09 PM||||Hi Kel,<P>Could the change in responsiveness be due to something as simple as tire wear? Months ago when I tried my balding rear tires on the front, my steering felt a whole lot less responsive (especially in the rain <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">).||||reg||1
Z||000002||kel||11-23-1999||07:46 PM||||I put 4 new tires on about 2 years ago.  They  have a little over 11,000 miles on them.  I guess I could get them rotated anyway and see if that makes a difference.||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||11-23-1999||07:50 PM||||There is two different racks. You have the quicker one already. You can adjust the rack to be tighter with the adjuster screw and locknut||||reg||1
Z||000004||kel||11-23-1999||08:22 PM||||TOM,<P>I know this is probably a little tedious for you guys, but could you state exactly where the adjuster screw and locknut are so I can pass on the info. in detail?  Thanks much.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||mwbackus||11-24-1999||03:01 AM||||Hmm..unless you have a ford rack in your Fiero, you would have a real hard time finding an adjusting nut. :-)<P>Some of the possible causes for your problem that I know of:<P>1.  Alignment; a must after strut replacement, and a challenge to find a shop that can do one correctly on a Fiero.<P>2.  Steering stablizer; a new one may be enough to give the feeling of less responsive steering, although it has actually just restored the "correct" steering feedback.<P>3.  Tires of course; but probably not an issure if correct balance and rotation has been observed on fairly new treads.<P>4.  Ball Joints;  an area of problem on nearly all higher milleage Fieros, more so with the 88's front lower ball joint "enhanced" failure interval.  Even a small amount of play in a ball joint is unacceptable.<P>5.  Outer tie rod ends; hard to detect when worn without separation/removal.<P>6.  Control arms; loose bolts or worn bushings can cause some very undesirable steering effects.<P>Given your description and its abrupt onset, I would conclude that an alignment problem is most likely.  It takes a few miles for tires to adjust to an alignment change, therefore, problems such as these may not appear for quite sometime after the initial alignment alterations have occured.<P>Mike<BR>(Fiero info in my user profile)||||reg||1
Z||000006||kel||11-27-1999||03:33 AM||||mwbackus,<P>I don't know if I'm describing this very well, but when I'm in a turn, I have to overcompensate to turn.  Then have to overcompensate again to straighten it out.  I hate it!  I am getting the tires rotated and an alignment probably this coming week.  I sure hope that does it!  If it doesn't, I'll be back...  Thanks for all the information.          ||||reg||1
Z||000007||kel||12-03-1999||03:50 PM||||Just an update... I had the 4 wheel alignment done and got the tires rotated.  I do feel somewhat of an improvement, but it's still not what it was.  Guess I'll live with it!  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||TOM||12-03-1999||03:55 PM||||Gee I dont have a ford rack and I adjusted mine tighter. ||||reg||1
