Z||000010||kel||12-03-1999||06:41 PM||||Yes, please tell me how, and where it is!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phaeton||12-04-1999||04:27 PM||||Factory alloy wheels have different backsets for front and rear, if you put the rear rims on the front or use aftermarket wheels having the same offset on all four you will not be able to get the steering to handle optimally.  Fieros are short and touchy about how far the tread centers are from the suspention pivot points, any wheel mods have to keep the same tread center to tread center widths.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||12-04-1999||04:40 PM||||Kel has a 86 SE, if she's still using the factory wheels, then I believe the wheel off-set is the same all around. Correct me if I'm wrong here guys, but wasn't the different off-set only used on the 88 models and maybe on the 87 GTs?<P>Lots of other things can cause problems, control arm bushings getting slightly mushy, a little wear here on the ball joints, some wear on the tie rod ends there, the steering rack starting to get a bit of wear, etc. Maybe all these combined, and possibly made worse by your accident, could start causing what you are describing. Tires can and do get out of round and out of balance too.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-04-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||12-04-1999||06:16 PM||||phaeton, only the 15 inch factory fiero wheel option uses diferant offsets/widths. All 13" and 14" Fiero factory wheels are the same for all 4 wheels. I think even the 14" "honey combs" used on some GT & SE are uniform offset and width.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Phaeton||12-04-1999||06:50 PM||||The steel factory wheels did have the same offset all around but it affects the handling.  I am running an 88 but I use 14" wheels for winter, and slick road driving shows handling probs imediately.  Giving the rear wheels an extra half inch out spacing over the fronts really improved error correction and predictability.  I spoke with another winter driver with an 86 and he found the same effects.  I wonder if this may be a factor only when driving with low traction and continually breaking traction.  I sometimes will have the rear broke loose for a half a block on long sweeping corners. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||fiero56||12-04-1999||07:59 PM||||I have an '88 also, and Ed Parks has told me the only reason they used different wheel offsets on the '88 is because of the new suspension that year recieved. Due to the geometry changes for '88, the front suspension acutally stuck out sideways an inch more than the previous years.  To compensate for these changes  the wheel offsets were changed. The Formula and GT got 15x7 rear, and 15x6 front. Not sure what the coupes got.||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||12-04-1999||10:08 PM||||Which 14" Ph. ? I can tell you for a fact that 87 and lower 14" "tech" wheels are all the same offset/width. 14x6 and +35mm off set as I recall. These wheels are pretty standard on all SC SE and GT prior to 88. These wheels are on so many pontiacs it's not funny. <P>There are the honey comb ones, but I've not got the specs handy for those. I think there may actually have been a couple diferant sets of those used.||||reg||1
Z||000017||kel||12-05-1999||03:45 PM||||I still have the original 14" factory wheels. (The same ones that are on that bogus "train wreck Fiero" picture.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>I was just wondering, could different brands of tires affect the feel of the steering?  I always had Goodyear on in previous years(Eagle GT+4 in '90 and ? Goodyear in '86) but this last time ('97) I had Firestone Firehawk put on.  Could it be that these are wearing differently and that's affecting the handling also?<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||batboy||12-05-1999||04:11 PM||||You bet Kel, tires can make or break handling quality. A tire that looks like it has good tread could have slipped or damaged internal belts. Look on the sidewalls, are there any readily visible dimples? This is one sign of a damaged belt. Also, they sometimes get out of round or out of balance. Maybe the accident knocked a wheel weight off or something. I'm not saying it is the tires, just that it could be.<P>Even different tread designs can effect handling differently. Tire companies constantly change their tread design and the rubber compounds. For example 4 year old Dunlap tire will be completely different than a new one of similar style.||||reg||1
Z||000019||kel||12-05-1999||04:33 PM||||I just checked them out.  I don't see or feel any dimples on the sidewalls at all.  When I had the tires rotated last week, they checked the wheel weights.  The tread still looks real good.<BR>Question:  On the tire, it states:<P>treadwear 400<BR>traction A<BR>temperature B<P>What does the 400 mean?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||RFMike||12-05-1999||07:16 PM||||You should feel fortunate.  I had a much longer response, but shortened it to this.  <P>The "400" refers to a treadwear rating.  Unfortunately, this rating varies from company to company, that is, a Michelin 400 rating may not be the same as a Firestone 400 rating.  Generally speaking, though, a 400 rating is usually a high mileage tire.  This type of tire is usually designed with a harder tread compound (longer life) and a sidewall with more give (the flex in the sidewall gives a more comfortable ride, and keeps the contact patch in more uniform contact with the road, which again gives longer life).  I'm betting you bought either the Firehawk SS10 or the Firehawk Touring LH, both of which are rated at 60,000.  If you push through corners, hit the curb when parking, and hit bumps hard, the sidewalls break down.  Especially if you have run them underinflated.  No offense, especially the curb part.  <BR>Your driveability description does sound like soft sidewalls.  Does it seem slow to respond to steering input?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||theogre||12-05-1999||07:47 PM||||Kel, As tires wear they will definatly change. As an example, in many classes of racing that require DOT legal tires it's common to put tire on a lathe and turn off half the tread or more.<P>Additionally every tire type, even from the same company is diferant. What Batboy says extends to tires that physically appear the same the same in every detail but are made even a few days apart. There is variation in every batch of tires, let allone every diferant type.<P>What RFmike says is correct in the real world. The tread wear number, in your case Tread=400, is based against a research reference tire. In the real world it has little meaning. The so called tire warrantee is only a bit more relative. longer warrantee means tire with harder tread compound. I've tryed these hard tires. I won't buy or recomend them on most cars. above about 35-40Kmile warantee the tread is so hard it bearly sticks to even dry pavement.<P>one thing you can do is measure the tread depth and compair that agains the original spec. That will give you an idea of where your at. I've found that tires change considerably someplace below 1/2 original tread. there are several reasons for this, but it's been found pretty true in most cases.<P>you can also check your air pressure very carfully. get a good digital, or dial type, gauge. Not one of those slag digitals K-mart wallmart and others carry. get a good one. expect to pay 30-50$us for a good digital. Good dial type are available at most parts store and run 10-20$us. I like the Dial type with the little "hold" button on the side, the button also allows easy bleed down to the pressure you want. Do Not rely on the cheapy stick gauges for handling work. Stick gauges are commonly 2PSI or more off.<P>Tire pressure is very critical. a .5-1psi diferance can make signifigant changes to handling.<P>First make sure all tires are the same pressure. start at the "door sticker" pressure. (for 195/70r14 that's 30PSI)<P>then add 2 or 3 psi to all 4 tires and see if it helps. (add the same amount to all 4)<P>I've found that in my 87SC that with it's current tires it likes 33 PSI. Your tires are likely to be a bit diferant. You probly don't need more than 34-35 absolute maximum. likely you only need a couple PSI above the sticker.||||reg||1
Z||000022||kel||12-05-1999||07:48 PM||||RFMike,  No offense taken.  I have the Firehawk SS10.  I curb park next to never, but when I do I'm very careful not to scrape.   I do mainly driving around town and know where just about every bump, railroad crossing and pothole is, and slow down for every one of them.  "Creep over them" is a better description.  As far as pushing through corners, I'm not quite sure what you mean.  If you mean taking them pretty fast...I'm definitely guilty of that.  I keep them inflated to specs., haven't checked them lately though - so I'll do that, but it does seem slow to respond to steering input.  I describe it as having to overcompensate for the turn going in and out of it.    ||||reg||1
Z||000023||kel||12-05-1999||08:03 PM||||Ogre,  These have a 50,000 mile warranty.  So I guess it's the "hard tire" type that you're describing.  If that's the case, I'm going to have a hard time convincing Mr. kel to get me new ones.  The tread on these are still really good.  I will do what you suggested with checking the tire pressure with a good gauge.  (I have to buy one first) and I'll keep you posted.  Thanks.||||reg||1
